{{/*
  shell — the whole page.

  The region containers below are the contract three things agree on: the
  partial envelope swaps into them by id (internal/app/partials.go), the
  carried stylesheet system files itself under their classes, and the seam
  reads the viewport's state node out of them. Each region's own template
  renders its own container, so a swap and a first paint produce the same
  bytes.

  Inheritance is declared once, here, and explicitly (issue #5 §4.3): every
  interaction on this page is about the same volume, so hx-vals says so at the
  top rather than every control repeating it, and nothing inherits by accident
  because nothing inherits without ":inherited" spelled out.
*/}}
{{define "shell"}}<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<meta name="color-scheme" content="dark">
<link rel="icon" href="data:,">
<title>{{.Title}} — Atlas</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/app.css">
<script src="/assets/htmx.js" defer></script>
</head>
<body class="atlas">
{{template "shell-region" .}}
{{template "import" .}}
{{/*
  The seam. It is loaded from /static, which is whatever tree the host mounted
  and answers 404 when it mounted none. An undefined <atlas-viewport> renders
  nothing and breaks nothing, which is the deletability principle costing this
  page one script tag (issue #5 §5.5).
*/}}
<script type="module" src="/static/app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
{{end}}

{{/*
  shell-region — the page frame as a region.

  The document above is what a first paint answers with; this is what a swap
  answers with, and they are the same bytes because the document renders this.
  It matters that they are separable: the sidebar's collapsed state is a class
  on this element, and a swap can only set an attribute on a container it
  renders *itself* -- so the shell is an outer morph over this, and the
  document is never a partial.
*/}}
{{define "shell-region"}}<div id="atlas-shell" class="app-shell{{if .Session.Sidebar.Collapsed}} sidebar-collapsed{{end}}"
     hx-vals:inherited='{"volume":"{{with .Volume}}{{.Slug}}{{end}}"}'
     hx-swap:inherited="none">
  <header id="atlas-topbar" class="topbar">{{template "topbar" .}}</header>
  {{template "legend" .}}
  <main class="map-panel">
    {{template "viewport-surface" .}}
    {{template "viewport" .}}
    <div class="zoom-controls" aria-label="Map controls">
      <button type="button" id="zoom-in" aria-label="Zoom in">+</button>
      <button type="button" id="zoom-out" aria-label="Zoom out">−</button>
    </div>
    {{template "grid-navigator" .}}
    <div class="map-corner">
      {{template "overview" .}}
      <div class="map-hint" id="map-hint">
        <span>Drag to pan</span><i></i>
        <span>Scroll to zoom</span><i></i>
        <span id="labels-hint">Z · hold for labels</span><i></i>
        <span id="grid-hint">{{if .Grid.Enabled}}G · {{.Grid.Name}}{{else}}G · grid off{{end}}</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  {{template "dock" .}}
  {{template "island" .}}
  {{/*
    The keyboard, as hypermedia.

    Every shortcut below moves discrete state, so every one of them is a route
    rather than a handler: an hx-trigger naming the key and the element the
    key is heard on, and nothing else. There is no hx-on here and no listener
    in the page -- the only shortcut this application does not spell out is Z,
    which holds the labels up while it is down and is continuous interaction
    state, so it belongs to the seam (issue #5 §4.1).

    They listen on the window rather than on the body, because that is where
    a keystroke with no focused control is heard: an event dispatched at the
    window never travels down to the body, so a shortcut listening there would
    hear a reader's own typing and nothing else.

    They are declared here rather than beside the controls they double for
    because a shortcut has to survive the region that would have carried it
    being swapped, and the shell is the one region a swap never replaces from
    underneath.
  */}}
  <div hidden id="atlas-shortcuts">
    <span hx-post="/session/grid" hx-vals:append='{"system":"toggle"}'
          hx-trigger="keydown[key=='g'&&!metaKey&&!ctrlKey&&!altKey] from:window"></span>
    <span hx-post="/session/grid" hx-vals:append='{"ascend":"1"}'
          hx-trigger="keydown[key=='Escape'] from:window"></span>
    <span hx-post="/session/grid" hx-vals:append='{"subgrid":"flip"}'
          hx-trigger="keydown[key==' '] from:window"></span>
    <span hx-post="/session/sidebar"
          hx-vals:append='{"open":"{{if .Session.Sidebar.Collapsed}}1{{else}}0{{end}}"}'
          hx-trigger="keydown[(metaKey||ctrlKey)&&key=='b'] from:window"></span>
  </div>
  {{/*
    The camera's way home.

    The seam reports a settled camera so a volume reopens where the reader
    left it (issue #5 §4.1). It is the one upward flow that is not a reader's
    move, and it is *this page's* post rather than the seam's own: the
    application renders the form and the fields, the seam fills them in and
    raises the event, and htmx does the rest. That is the same arrangement the
    labels hint and the corner locator already stand in -- chrome the
    application renders for the seam to draw into (docs/render-seam.md §2) --
    and it is what lets the answer land. A report the seam posted for itself
    would throw the answer away, and the answer is the state island carrying
    the camera that was just written.
  */}}
  <form hidden id="atlas-camera" hx-post="/session/view"
        hx-trigger="atlas:camera from:window">
    <input type="hidden" name="world" id="atlas-camera-world"
           value="{{with .Volume}}{{.World.Slug}}{{end}}">
    <input type="hidden" name="x" id="atlas-camera-x">
    <input type="hidden" name="y" id="atlas-camera-y">
    <input type="hidden" name="zoom" id="atlas-camera-zoom">
    <input type="hidden" name="rotation" id="atlas-camera-rotation" value="0">
  </form>
  {{/*
    The pick's way in.

    The panes resolve a hit themselves -- a pixel is theirs and an identity is
    this application's (docs/render-seam.md §5) -- and this is the half that
    was missing: the seam handed an identity up and nothing was standing there
    to take it, so a click on the canvas moved nothing at all.

    It is the camera's arrangement exactly, and for the same reason. The
    application renders the form and the field it wants filled, the seam fills
    it in and raises the event, htmx posts it and applies the answer -- and the
    answer is the selection the legend marks and the dock and the card draw,
    which are the page's regions, not the seam's. A request the seam made for
    itself would throw all of that away.

    ONE FIELD, and the two absences are the point. The volume is not here
    because it is inherited from the shell above like every other interaction's
    on this page, and `focus` is not here because a pick off the canvas is not
    a jump: a row reached for from a list marks the ground it opened in the
    index, and a reader who clicked the ground was already standing on it
    (applySelect, internal/app/session.go).
  */}}
  <form hidden id="atlas-pick" hx-post="/session/select"
        hx-trigger="atlas:pick from:window">
    <input type="hidden" name="feature" id="atlas-pick-feature">
  </form>
  {{/*
    Live updates. The stream carries ready-to-swap partials and two event
    names (docs/app.md §5); the page takes the plainer road of re-fetching
    itself on either, because both events mean the library moved under it and
    a page that re-reads its own URL cannot end up half-patched. The bodies
    stay in the contract for a client that wants the cheaper path.

    These stand inside #atlas-shell for the same reason the island does: the
    address they re-fetch is the address of the volume they were rendered
    for, and a pair left outside the shell goes on naming the volume the page
    first opened on. A reconcile would then take the reader somewhere else,
    which is exactly what an unchanged catalog must never do.
  */}}
  {{with .Volume}}<div hidden id="atlas-events" hx-sse:connect="/events?volume={{.Slug}}" hx-trigger="load"></div>
  <div hidden hx-get="/v/{{.Slug}}/{{.World.Slug}}"
       hx-trigger="catalog from:#atlas-events, refresh from:#atlas-events"
       hx-target="#atlas-shell" hx-select="#atlas-shell" hx-swap="outerMorph"></div>{{end}}
</div>
{{end}}
